templ gettingStartedHowItWorks() {
	@docLead() {
		Breadboard reads one file, works out everything that file implies, writes a Docker Compose project, and runs it. This page follows that path from @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml") to running containers.
	}
	@docH2("engine", "One engine, thin wrappers")
	@docP() {
		Every capability lives in one core engine. The command line, the web UI (started with @docCodeWord("bb serve")) and the MCP server for AI agents (started with @docCodeWord("bb mcp")) are thin wrappers: each turns its input into a call to the engine and renders the result. A wrapper holds no logic of its own, so anything the CLI can do, the other front ends get by calling the same operation.
	}
	@docP() {
		All of this is one Go binary on your machine. Breadboard itself never runs in a container. The web UI only reads: it shows topology, health, logs and what each service holds, and gives you the @docCodeWord("bb") command to run when you want to change something.
	}
	@docH2("source", "experiment.yaml is the source of truth")
	@docP() {
		There is no database. @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml") holds your intent: which services, which apps, what each app uses, and any TLS exceptions. It never holds secrets, certificates or generated values, so it is safe to commit. Everything it leaves out takes a catalog default.
	}
	@docP() {
		Every output is generated from it. The engine never reads a generated compose file back as input, so editing the compose file by hand changes nothing: the next @docCodeWord("bb up") writes it again from the spec.
	}
	@docH2("up", "What bb up does")
	@docSteps(
		"Check that Docker is reachable and take the project's lock, .bb/lock, so two commands never change one experiment at the same time. A second caller gets a busy error naming the operation in progress.",
		"Load experiment.yaml and validate it, reporting every violation with its YAML path. Each app's path must hold a Dockerfile with a stage named dev.",
		"Resolve the spec against the catalog into the resolved model: defaults applied, edges derived from uses, listeners and network segments computed.",
		"Reconcile state: create the CA if it is missing, generate any missing secrets, issue or reissue leaf certificates, and allocate subnets for private segments.",
		"Write the compose project into .bb/runtime/: docker-compose.yml, the trust bundle, and each service's certificate and configuration files. Files whose content has not changed are left in place, because running containers bind-mount them.",
		"Run docker compose up --detach --build --remove-orphans under the project name bb-<experiment>. App images build here; after you edit code, the next bb up rebuilds the image and recreates the container.",
		"Wait up to 5 minutes (--timeout) for every container to be running and healthy, then require all of them to stay healthy for a 5-second settle window.",
	)
	@docCallout("Why the settle window") {
		A program can crash right after its first health check passes. So @docCodeWord("bb up") also tracks each container's restart count and start time, and fails if any container exits, reports unhealthy, or restarts at any point during the wait. The error names the service and points you at @docCodeWord("bb logs")&#46;
	}
	@docP() {
		Health has a precise meaning. Infra services use the check from their catalog entry. An app's default check is that its main process is running, so app code needs no health endpoint; an app can opt into an HTTP check with @docCodeWord("health") in the spec.
	}
	@docTerminal("bb up", "bb status")
	@docH2("down", "Stopping and resetting")
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("bb down") runs @docCodeWord("docker compose down") on the experiment and also removes networks labeled with the experiment's id, so networks of services you removed since an earlier @docCodeWord("bb up") do not linger. Data volumes stay, so the next @docCodeWord("bb up") finds your topics, tables and keys where you left them.
	}
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("bb down --volumes") deletes the data volumes too. Secrets are kept even then, because they live in @docCodeWord(".bb/")&#46; To reset an experiment completely, run @docCodeWord("bb down --volumes") and delete @docCodeWord(".bb/"): the next @docCodeWord("bb up") generates fresh secrets and certificates.
	}
	@docP() {
		Every container, network and volume carries the experiment's id as a label. If another directory holds an experiment with the same name, @docCodeWord("bb up") and @docCodeWord("bb down") refuse to act rather than touch its resources.
	}
	@docH2("state", "Where state lives")
	@docList(
		"experiment.yaml and your app code: in the project directory. Yours, committed.",
		".bb/ beside experiment.yaml: secrets.json, the leaf certificates in certs/, state.json, the lock, and the generated compose project in runtime/. Created by bb init, never committed (it holds its own .gitignore).",
		"BB_HOME: install settings in config.yaml and the CA in ca/. It defaults to your user config directory plus breadboard (~/Library/Application Support/breadboard on macOS) and can be moved with the BB_HOME environment variable.",
	)
	@docP() {
		Every file Breadboard owns carries a format version and is upgraded forward on load. A @docCodeWord("bb") that meets a newer format than it knows refuses to write and asks you to upgrade. @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml") is the exception: Breadboard never rewrites it silently, and @docCodeWord("bb migrate") upgrades it when you ask.
	}
}

templ gettingStartedExperiments() {
	@docLead() {
		An experiment is a directory with an @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml"), your app code, and Breadboard's @docCodeWord(".bb/") state. It becomes one compose project with its own networks.
	}
	@docH2("file", "The file")
	@docP() {
		The file has four top-level keys. @docCodeWord("version") is the schema version, currently 1. @docCodeWord("name") is a DNS label of up to 40 characters: lower-case letters, digits and hyphens; it names the compose project and is part of every service's identity. @docCodeWord("services") are infra services from the catalog. @docCodeWord("apps") are your code.
	}
	@docP() {
		Service and app names share one namespace and must be unique DNS labels, because each becomes an address on the experiment's network.
	}
	@docTerminal("bb init kafka-lab")
	@docH2("example", "An annotated example")
	@docCode("experiment.yaml", gsExperimentYAML)
	@docList(
		"type: a catalog entry id. bb catalog lists them.",
		"resources: memory as a Docker size string and cpus as a decimal. Apps default to 256m and 0.5 cpus; infra services take their catalog entry's defaults.",
		"config: options a catalog entry defines, such as the Postgres database name, validated by that entry.",
		"path: relative to experiment.yaml. Its Dockerfile needs a dev stage; compile the app there and run the binary as the command, as the starters do.",
		"ports: container ports to publish. Nothing is published to your machine unless you ask, and then only on 127.0.0.1.",
		"register: a Consul sidecar named greeter-agent registers the app, so its code does not register itself. It needs a registry service in uses.",
	)
	@docH2("uses", "uses creates edges")
	@docP() {
		Each @docCodeWord("uses") entry is an edge from the app to an infra service. An edge does three things: it injects that service's environment variables into the app, it adds a @docCodeWord("depends_on") with @docCodeWord("condition: service_healthy") so the app starts after the service is healthy, and it carries the edge's TLS policy. This is what Breadboard generates for the producer above:
	}
	@docCode(".bb/runtime/docker-compose.yml", gsComposeExcerpt)
	@docP() {
		The short form, a service name, means TLS with full verification. The long form, an object with @docCodeWord("service") and @docCodeWord("tls"), is how one edge deviates; see @docLink("/docs/getting-started/tls-by-default", "TLS by default")&#46; When an app uses two services that share a variable name, such as two Postgres services, the later one's variables are prefixed with its name, for example @docCodeWord("ANALYTICS_DATABASE_URL")&#46;
	}
	@docH2("validation", "Validation")
	@docP() {
		Validation reports every violation at once, each with its YAML path, before anything runs.
	}
	@docList(
		"Unknown keys are errors, so a typo never passes silently.",
		"Every uses target must exist and be an infra service, listed once.",
		"tls.mode is on or off. off needs a non-empty reason, and is allowed only toward entries with a plaintext listener (Redis and S3 Compatible have none).",
		"Fields reserved for later phases, such as tls.clientAuth for mTLS, are rejected with the phase that introduces them.",
	)
	@docH2("commands", "Working with an experiment")
	@docP() {
		You can edit @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml") by hand. The commands that change it edit a YAML node tree, so your comments and key order survive. Commands find the experiment by walking up from the current directory to the nearest @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml")&#46;
	}
	@docList(
		"bb init [name]: create experiment.yaml and .bb/ here; create BB_HOME and the CA if missing.",
		"bb add <type> [--name n]: add an infra service, named after its type by default.",
		"bb remove <name>: remove a service or app. A service an app still uses is refused until you remove it from that app's uses.",
		"bb starter <id> [--dir path]: write a starter's code and add its apps.",
		"bb up and bb down [--volumes]: start, or stop and optionally delete data.",
		"bb status: each service's state, health, CPU and memory, plus warnings for plaintext edges.",
		"bb logs [service] [-f] [--tail n]: stream logs.",
	)
	@docTerminal("bb add postgres", "bb up", "bb status", "bb logs consumer -f")
}

templ gettingStartedTLS() {
	@docLead() {
		Every connection between containers uses TLS with full verification, and you write nothing to get it. Plaintext is possible, but only as an explicit choice with a written reason, and it is always shown.
	}
	@docH2("ca", "One CA per install")
	@docP() {
		The first time it is needed, Breadboard creates a certificate authority with Go's standard library (ECDSA P-256) and stores it in @docCodeWord("BB_HOME/ca/"), with the key readable only by you. Every experiment on the machine shares it. It is valid for 5 years.
	}
	@docP() {
		The CA is name-constrained to the install's domain, @docCodeWord("bb.internal") by default. The constraint is marked critical, so even a leaked CA key cannot mint a certificate a client would accept for any real site. The domain must end in a reserved suffix such as @docCodeWord(".internal") or @docCodeWord(".test"), which never resolves publicly. Changing the domain mints a new CA and reissues every certificate on the next @docCodeWord("bb up")&#46;
	}
	@docH2("leaves", "A certificate per service")
	@docP() {
		Every infra service and every app gets its own certificate, issued at @docCodeWord("bb up"):
	}
	@docList(
		"DNS name: <service>.<domain>, for example postgres.bb.internal, which is also the service's network alias. Connection strings use this name; the bare name postgres resolves but is not on the certificate.",
		"URI: the service's identity, spiffe://<domain>/experiment/<exp>/service/<svc>.",
		"Usage: both server and client authentication, so apps already hold certificates when mTLS arrives in a later phase.",
		"Lifetime: 90 days. bb up reissues a certificate when fewer than 30 days remain, or when it no longer matches the CA, the service's name or its identity.",
	)
	@docP() {
		A reissued certificate changes the service's @docCodeWord("breadboard.config-hash") label, so Compose recreates that container and it starts with the new certificate.
	}
	@docH2("trust", "How apps trust and serve")
	@docP() {
		Each container gets read-only files under @docCodeWord("/etc/bb/"): the trust bundle (the Breadboard CA followed by your machine's public roots), the CA alone, and its own certificate and key. Every app also receives these variables:
	}
	@docList(
		"SSL_CERT_FILE=/etc/bb/trust-bundle.pem. Go's TLS stack reads it, so pgx, go-redis, franz-go and the Consul API verify Breadboard certificates with no code changes, and TLS to the internet still works.",
		"TLS_CERT_FILE and TLS_KEY_FILE: the app's own certificate and key, for apps that serve TLS to other apps.",
		"BB_SERVICE and BB_FQDN: the app's name and its address, the name on its certificate.",
		"Per edge: the used service's env contract, such as KAFKA_BROKERS or DATABASE_URL, already pointing at the TLS listener.",
	)
	@docCode("cmd/sink/main.go", gsPgxCode)
	@docP() {
		An app that serves HTTPS passes its certificate paths from the environment, so no path appears in code. This is the consul/service-discovery starter's greeter:
	}
	@docCode("cmd/greeter/main.go", gsServeTLSCode)
	@docH2("plaintext", "Plaintext on purpose")
	@docP() {
		TLS is decided per edge, because proxies and relays make a decision at each hop. To turn it off for one edge, use the long form of @docCodeWord("uses") with a reason:
	}
	@docCode("experiment.yaml", gsPlaintextYAML)
	@docP() {
		What actually happens: the target turns on a plaintext listener used only by edges that asked for it, and Breadboard puts that listener on a private segment, an internal compose network named @docCodeWord("plain-postgres"), shared only with those clients. The target gets the address .10 on the segment and the alias @docCodeWord("postgres.plain.<domain>"); the app's variables point there instead, with @docCodeWord("sslmode=disable")&#46; Kafka and Consul bind their plaintext listener to that address; Postgres accepts unencrypted connections only from the segment's subnet. Redis and S3 Compatible cannot do either, so they offer no plaintext listener.
	}
	@docFigure("The producer reaches Kafka over TLS on the shared network. The consumer's plaintext edge to Postgres exists only on the private segment.") {
		@gettingStartedTLSDiagram()
	}
	@docP() {
		The edge is never hidden: @docCodeWord("bb up") and @docCodeWord("bb status") print it as a warning with its reason, the web UI draws it dashed red, and an exported compose file carries the reason as a comment.
	}
	@docH2("host", "Trusting the CA on your machine")
	@docP() {
		Containers already trust the CA. Your machine does not, and Breadboard never adds it to your trust store. @docCodeWord("bb ca") shows the CA's subject, the domain it is constrained to, its validity and fingerprint, and the commands to trust it on macOS or Debian and Ubuntu if you choose to. @docCodeWord("bb ca --pem") prints the certificate itself.
	}
	@docP() {
		You do not need to trust it to open lab services in a browser: @docLink("/docs/getting-started/host-access", "Host access") serves them through a router whose certificate comes from a separate CA that can sign only @docCodeWord("*.localhost") names.
	}
	@docTerminal("bb ca", "bb ca --pem > breadboard-ca.pem")
}

templ gettingStartedTLSDiagram() {
	<svg viewBox="0 0 700 230" width="700" class="mx-auto block h-auto max-w-full" role="img" aria-label="producer connects to kafka over TLS on port 9093; consumer connects to postgres in plaintext on port 5432, inside the private segment plain-postgres, drawn dashed red">
		<defs>
			<marker id="gs-tls-arrow" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="fill-slate-400"></path></marker>
			<marker id="gs-plain-arrow" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="fill-red-600"></path></marker>
		</defs>
		<rect x="40" y="30" width="150" height="40" rx="6" class="fill-white stroke-slate-200"></rect>
		<text x="115" y="55" text-anchor="middle" class="fill-slate-900 text-[13px] font-semibold">producer</text>
		<line x1="190" y1="50" x2="500" y2="50" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#gs-tls-arrow)" class="stroke-slate-400"></line>
		<rect x="300" y="40" width="96" height="20" rx="4" class="fill-white stroke-slate-300"></rect>
		<text x="348" y="54" text-anchor="middle" class="fill-slate-600 font-mono text-[11px]">TLS :9093</text>
