}

templ gettingStartedStarters() {
	@docLead() {
		A starter takes you from "I want to try Kafka consumers" to running code in minutes. It writes a small, working Go project into your experiment, adds its apps to @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml"), and adds any infra service it needs that is missing. After that, the code is yours.
	}
	@docH2("what", "What a starter writes")
	@docList(
		"One Go module, with one main.go per app under cmd/<app>. Each app is the least code that shows the concept, using the official client library directly.",
		"A multi-stage Dockerfile: a dev stage that bb up runs, and a prod stage for building a small release image by hand.",
		"A README with two sections: Try this, guided experiments, and Break this, failure drills such as killing the broker.",
		"Entries in experiment.yaml: one app per cmd/<app>, with command set to its binary and uses pointing at the experiment's service of each type it needs.",
	)
	@docP() {
		The code reads only environment variables from the catalog's env contract, such as @docCodeWord("KAFKA_BROKERS") or @docCodeWord("DATABASE_URL"), and verifies TLS through @docCodeWord("SSL_CERT_FILE")&#46; There are no Breadboard config files and no certificate paths in code, so the project runs anywhere those variables and a trust bundle exist.
	}
	@docH2("build", "How starter apps run")
	@docP() {
		Apps are compiled while the image builds, not when the container starts. The dev stage downloads dependencies and builds every app into @docCodeWord("/usr/local/bin/"), with a BuildKit cache mount that keeps compiled packages between builds:
	}
	@docCode("Dockerfile", gsDockerfile)
	@docP() {
		Each app's @docCodeWord("command") is its binary's name, so the program is the container's main process, PID 1. It receives SIGTERM and runs its shutdown code, and when its health check passes the program itself is running, not a compiler. There is no source mount: after you edit code, @docCodeWord("bb up") rebuilds the image, recompiling only what changed, and recreates the container.
	}
	@docH2("apply", "Applying a starter")
	@docTerminal("bb starter kafka/producer-consumer", "bb starter kafka/sink-to-postgres --dir orders-sink")
	@docP() {
		The code goes into a directory inside the project named after the starter, such as @docCodeWord("./producer-consumer"), or wherever @docCodeWord("--dir") says. The spec changes are validated before any file is written.
	}
	@docList(
		"Re-applying never overwrites your edits. A file that matches the starter is left alone; a file you changed is reported as a conflict and kept as it is; only missing files are written.",
		"App names are unique across every starter, so several starters can share one experiment. Re-applying a starter to the same directory leaves its existing apps as you left them.",
		"If a name the starter needs already belongs to something else in experiment.yaml, bb starter fails before writing any file.",
	)
	@docH2("list", "The starters")
	<ul class="my-4 flex flex-col gap-2.5 text-[16px] leading-7 text-slate-700">
		for _, s := range gsStarterList {
			<li class="flex gap-3">
				<span class="text-slate-400">—</span>
				<span>
					@docLink("/starters/"+s.ID, s.ID)
					<span>{ ": " + s.Teaches }</span>
				</span>
			</li>
		}
	</ul>
	@docP() {
		Each technology's docs go deeper: @docLink("/docs/kafka", "Kafka"), @docLink("/docs/postgres", "Postgres"), @docLink("/docs/redis", "Redis"), @docLink("/docs/consul", "Consul") and @docLink("/docs/s3-compatible", "S3 Compatible")&#46;
	}
}

// gsStarterList is the starter list in starters.md, in its order.
var gsStarterList = []struct{ ID, Teaches string }{
	{"kafka/producer-consumer", "topics, partitions, keys, consumer groups, offsets and rebalancing."},
	{"kafka/sink-to-postgres", "consume events and write them to Postgres; idempotent inserts; at-least-once delivery."},
	{"redis/cache-invalidation", "cache-aside reads, invalidate-on-write driven by Kafka events, TTLs and stale reads."},
	{"consul/service-discovery", "(recommended) registration through a Consul sidecar, a health-checked catalog, and resolving services by name."},
	{"consul/self-registration", "(alternative) the same lookup, with each service registering and deregistering itself through the Consul API."},
	{"s3/objects-basics", "buckets, keys and prefixes, multipart upload and presigned URLs against S3 Compatible storage."},
}
